    {
      "id": "cn-northern-southern",
      "name": "The Northern and Southern dynasties",
      "start": 420,
      "end": 589,
      "region": "divided",
      "icon": "🏯",
      "description": "For 170 years China was split along the Huai River. In the south, the Liu Song (420), Southern Qi (479), Liang (502) and Chen (557) ruled from Jiankang (Nanjing), a centre of Buddhism, poetry and aristocratic culture; Emperor Wu of Liang reigned for 48 years before Hou Jing’s rebellion (548–552). In the north, the Xianbei Northern Wei moved its capital from Pingcheng to Luoyang (494) and adopted Chinese ways; it split in 534 into Eastern Wei/Northern Qi and Western Wei/Northern Zhou. The Northern Zhou conquered the Qi (577), and its successor the Sui took Chen in 589.",
